\subsection{Supporting Diagnostic Analyses}

Four additional analyses confirm the mechanism underlying SICA's failure. Extraction error taxonomy (Appendix~\ref{app:extraction}): 91.8\% of constraints are technically discriminative, but their discriminative direction mirrors the majority answer; removing the 8.2\% non-discriminative constraints yields zero improvement. Constraint ablation (Appendix~\ref{app:ablation}): on Mistral-7B ($n = 804$, FOLIO + ProofWriter pooled), MAX-SAT and count-only agree on 94.4\% of predictions, with disagreements split nearly evenly (18 vs.\ 17); the solver's weighted satisfiability computation adds no value over simple constraint counting because constraint weights carry information redundant with vote counts (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}). Per-domain, MAX-SAT provides a marginal edge in the intermediate-SC region (59.3\% vs.\ count-only 56.9\%) but actively hurts in the Type~C regime (36.5\% vs.\ 37.2\%), mirroring the regime-dependent pattern of SICA itself. Hyperparameter sensitivity (Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity}): $K = 12$ is the only setting with $\Delta > 2$\,pp among five $K$ values, and only $T = 0.7$ among four temperatures yields a positive effect; both peaks coincide with relatively low SC baselines rather than reflecting hyperparameter effects per se. Entropy decomposition (Appendix~\ref{app:entropy}): all SICA effects concentrate in high-entropy problems ($\Delta$ up to $+12.8$\,pp in the intermediate-SC region), but the direction depends on the accuracy regime---high entropy signals correctable uncertainty only when the model has partial knowledge.

\subsection{Remediation Analysis}
\label{sec:remediation}
\label{sec:interventions}

We test twelve remediation strategies: nine prompt-level spanning six dimensions (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:remediation}) and three method-level (Table~\ref{tab:method_interventions}). All nine prompt-level strategies fail. Contrastive prompting reduces non-discriminative constraints from 8.2\% to 6.0\% but newly discriminative constraints still mirror the majority ($\Delta = -1.1$\,pp). Cross-model extraction fails in both directions: weaker models extracting from stronger traces yield 0\% discriminative constraints; stronger models extracting from weaker traces produce accuracy within $\pm$1\,pp of self-extraction. Only 1 of 4 temperature settings yields a positive effect ($p = 0.092$); multi-temperature sampling produces $\Delta = 0.00$\,pp.

\paragraph{Method-level interventions.} Three method-level strategies target different aspects of the independence bottleneck on Mistral-7B (Table~\ref{tab:method_interventions}). Independent Constraint Generation (ICG) bypasses trace-constraint coupling by generating constraints directly from the problem statement, producing near-zero average effect ($\Delta = -1.96$\,pp and $+1.47$\,pp across two FOLIO seeds; $-0.50$\,pp on LogiQA). Contrastive scoring generates pro and con constraints for each candidate but yields $\Delta = -9.31$\,pp on FOLIO and $-4.50$\,pp on LogiQA, with ${\sim}$21\% degenerate scores on both datasets. Debate adds a proposer-critic-defense chain, producing $\Delta = -18.63$\,pp on FOLIO and $-23.00$\,pp on LogiQA ($n = 200$)---the largest degradation across all twelve strategies on both datasets. At the 7B scale, multi-role prompting adds cognitive overhead that degrades both reasoning and extraction quality.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Method-level interventions on Mistral-7B. Each targets a different aspect of the independence bottleneck. All produce neutral-to-catastrophic results. ICG shows two FOLIO seeds.}
\label{tab:method_interventions}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrr@{}}
\toprule
Method & Target & FOLIO $\Delta$ (pp) & LogiQA $\Delta$ (pp) \\
\midrule
SC baseline & --- & 0.00 & 0.00 \\
ICG & Trace coupling & $-$1.96 / $+$1.47 & $-$0.50 \\
Contrastive & Scoring bias & $\mathbf{-9.31}$ & $-$4.50 \\
Debate & Role diversity & $\mathbf{-18.63}$ & $\mathbf{-23.00}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Independence-accuracy trade-off.} Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization} compares Fleiss' $\kappa$ across three aggregation methods on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$). Self-Consistency produces $\kappa = 0.44$ (Eff-$K = 2.02$). Diverse-Prompt achieves $\kappa = 0.40$ (Eff-$K = 2.21$): prompt variation does not break the underlying correlation. Debate achieves $\kappa = 0.15$ (Eff-$K = 4.44$), nearly tripling the effective sample size, but collapses accuracy by $-18.6$\,pp. This constitutes a Pareto frontier: methods that increase trace independence destroy accuracy, while methods that preserve accuracy cannot break the correlation floor. The only demonstrated path past this frontier requires signal from outside the model's distribution, consistent with the oracle result (\S\ref{sec:oracle}) and published systems that bypass self-extraction \citep{xu2026dylos, li2025aristotle, wang2025fover, pan2023logiclm}.

